$\mathrm{NaCl}$ on dissociation gives $\mathrm{Na}^{+}$and $\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$ions as follows:

\underset{\text { Sodlum chloride }}{\mathrm{NaCl}(a q)} \longrightarrow \underset{\text { Common ions }}{\mathrm{Na}^{+}}+\mathrm{Cl}^{-} \text {(ions) }

While, soap in the solution gives $\mathrm{RCOO}^{-}$and $\mathrm{Na}^{+}$ions as follows:

R-\mathrm{COONa}(s) \longrightarrow \mathrm{RCOO}^{-}+\mathrm{Na}^{+}

where $R-\mathrm{COONa}(s)$ is soap
$\therefore$ Solubility of soap decrease due to common ion $\left(\mathrm{Na}^{+}\right)$ present in the solution due to Le-Chatelier's principle, reaction shifts towards the reactant side.
